==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Rover Cat Mk ll''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Catellite''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
Rover Cat is a useful meatshield in both [[Into the Future]] and [[Cats of the Cosmos]] thanks to its high strength against Aliens and its immunity to warp. At high levels, it can tank many hits from even [[Star Peng]]s and its attack power allows it to break [[General GreGory]]/[[Corporal Weyland]]'s [[Barrier]].

Works very well with "Research" Effect UP [[Cat Combo]]s like [[Biohazard (Combo)|Biohazard]] and [[Bony Bone (Combo)|Bony Bone]], which are able to make it a somewhat reliable defensive unit. Despite its stats and abilities, it shouldn't be relied on as your only meatshield against strong Alien threats.

==='''True Form'''===
Catellite gains immunities that can help against [[Cli-One]], [[Calamary]], [[Croakley]] and [[Queen B]]. Otherwise, the lack of better health and the very small cost improvement make this True Form only situationally better than before, so it can be put on low priority in favor of cats who improve more drastically. Its lower cost can be seen as a bonus, however certain [[Restriction Stage]]s prevent the use of units below 300¢. In such cases, switching to Rover Cat MK II is recommended.

==='''Talents'''===
To start off, Barrier Breaker is useless, if not outright detrimental to have on Catellite. Catellite can already deal enough damage to bust through most Starred Aliens' Barriers, and much better options exist for those it can't. [[Fencer Cat (Super Rare Cat)|The Kitty of Liberty]], [[Li'l Gross Cat (Special Cat)|Li'l Macho Legs]] and the [[Ototo Corps|Breakerblast Cannon]] are just some of the examples for that, and unlike Catellite, none of them require any NP be spent for them to be effective in this field. Moreover, it prevents Catellite from being able to perma-stall enemies like [[UltraBaaBaa]] and [[Youcan]], which are frequently used as a way to build up large stacks of Units for [[Cheese|cheesing]] levels, although this is better performed using [[Curling Cat (Rare Cat)|Cleanerbot Cat]]/[[Curling Cat (Rare Cat)|Green Shell Cat]] or [[Ancient Egg: N108 (Super Rare Cat)|Soap Cat]], if you have advanced enough in the game. Only unlock it if you feel like burning some NP, as even from the most positive point of view, it's not worth it.

Due to the lack of mixed stages where the Relics present are weak enough to not immediately kill Catellite or leave it in OHKO range, Curse Immunity should be treated as a very low priority Talent. Only consider this if your Catellite has a high amount of + levels. Even then, though, it can easily be ignored without there being much of any consequence for doing so.

While Resist Knockback is likely intended for enemies such as [[Scissoroo]] and [[Nimoy Bore]], the NP cost for this simply isn't worth the miniscule effect it has on their pushing power. Catellite already has enough speed to recover nearly all of the ground lost to a Scissoroo's Knockback before she moves again, while Nimoy Bore's fast attack (and thereby proc) rate would effectively nullify the benefits of this ability in most scenarios. In addition, those two enemies are also very susceptible to being crowd controlled by units like [[Surfer Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Seafarer]] and [[Bakery Cat (Rare Cat)|Fried Chicken Cat]], further reducing the need for this Talent. As for the other Aliens with Knockback, their attacks are too infrequent for Resist Knockback to have any notable effect against them.

Both stat buff Talents are worth investing in at least. HP Buff of course is a must-have, given that Catellite's main role is tanking enemies, while Attack Buff can serve to improve its already impressive damage for a meatshield.

==='''Orb'''===
Catallite generally relies on health, rather than damage, so Defence Up Alien can be seen as superior to Strong vs. Up Alien. However, the latter can still be used effectively if the player wishes to improve Catellite's DPS.

